NAME
    cores - list, download, install, update, roll back, remove and
    verify the libretro cores EmuSen hosts

SYNOPSIS
    cores ls [--installed|--available|--updates|--unsupported] [<console>]
    cores info <core>
    cores fetch
    cores get <core>...
    cores update [<core>...]
    cores rollback <core>
    cores rm <core>...
    cores verify [<core>...]
    cores approve <core>
    cores default <console> [<engine>]
    cores default --game <path> [<engine>|-]
    cores wait [cores<n>]

DESCRIPTION
    The command of the emusen.cores module, the shell's face of the core
    tool; the Cores window is its other face, over the same jobs. It
    reaches the tool through the emusen.library service, version 1.5 or
    later, whose requests beginning core- the platform library hands to
    the core tool.

    EmuSen hosts a libretro core for each console it has a core of its
    own for, plus the Saturn and the Dreamcast: pcee2 (PlayStation 2),
    flycast (Dreamcast, Naomi, Atomiswave), snes9x, nestopia,
    genesis_plus_gx (Genesis, Sega CD, Master System, SG-1000),
    picodrive (32X), mupen64plus_next, sameboy, mednafen_psx_hw and
    mednafen_saturn. Only they are listed for download, and a core put
    in /Cores/libretro that is not one of them is listed as unsupported,
    with the reason. Snes9x, Genesis Plus GX and PicoDrive are under
    non-commercial licences, shown by cores info before download.

    A console's own core stays its default; a hosted core is chosen with
    cores default. The PlayStation 2 is the one exception: pcee2 is its
    default while Sapphire is under development.

    A core is named by its file's stem: pcee2 for pcee2_libretro.so. The
    stem names the core's folder under /Cores/libretro and its files; the
    core's own corename (LRPS2 for pcsx2) is shown, and names nothing.

    NOTHING IS FETCHED UNLESS ASKED. cores fetch is the only network
    request: it reads libretro's core information archive
    (assets/frontend/info.zip) and this platform's nightly index
    (nightly/linux/x86_64/latest/.index-extended) from
    buildbot.libretro.com over HTTPS, the certificate checked against the
    system's own store, a redirect to any other host refused. Both are
    checked before the cached copies in /Cores/libretro/.catalogue/ are
    replaced, so a fetch that fails leaves the catalogue as it was. cores
    ls and cores info read the cached copies, and work offline.

    cores ls prints one line per core: state, console, id, source,
    version, size and licence, separated by spaces, an empty field as -
    and spaces inside a field as _, so that awk and cut can take them
    apart; the licence is the rest of the line. The states:

        installed     downloaded, and its index line unchanged
        update        downloaded, and the index offers another build
        available     in the catalogue, not downloaded
        unsupported   EmuSen's host cannot run it: a core needing only
                      OpenGL or Direct3D, or one for a system no EmuSen
                      console runs; cores info gives the reason
        added         a folder under /Cores/libretro without a NOTICE,
                      put there by the player; never removed by Cores

    Whether an installed core is approved, waits for approval, or needs
    refreshing after EmuSen was updated is the runtime's trust, shown in
    the Cores window.

    cores info prints what the window's detail pane shows, as key: value
    lines: the names and authors, the licence, the version and build,
    the systems, the notes, the permissions, the firmware it names (the
    player's own, which nothing fetches), and where the build comes from
    with the index's CRC-32; for an installed core also its SHA-256 and
    the date it was fetched.

INSTALLING
    Nothing is downloaded unless asked: cores get and cores update are
    the only commands besides cores fetch that make a network request,
    each to buildbot.libretro.com alone, over HTTPS. A download is held
    to every check before anything is kept, and each check prints
    nothing when it passes; the first that fails refuses the core and
    says why:

        transport      HTTPS, the certificate checked against the
                       system's store, a redirect off the buildbot
                       refused
        crc32          the core's CRC-32 as the zip records it equals
                       the index line the plan was made from (the
                       index gives the library's, not the zip's); a
                       difference means the build changed since
                       cores fetch, or the bytes did
        shape          exactly one member, named <core>_libretro.so
                       whole: no folder, no .., no absolute path, no
                       link, no other file
        size           at most 512 MB as the zip declares it, as it
                       inflates (stopped at the bound) and as the zip
        members        the member's CRC-32 and length as the zip
                       records them
        architecture   the library's header names this machine
        sha256         the core's SHA-256, written to its NOTICE

    The core is unpacked in /Cores/libretro/.staging/, with its core
    information, its NOTICE and copies of this EmuSen's libretro host
    and proxy, and put in place by one rename, so a core is either
    installed whole or not at all. A build of EmuSen without the
    libretro host installs nothing, and says so.

    NOTHING DOWNLOADED RUNS UNTIL YOU APPROVE IT. cores get prints the
    SHA-256 to approve, and cores approve <core> approves it, typed at
    the prompt only: a script given to source, a $(...) and a pipe are
    refused, as trust refuses them. libretro signs no build, so the approval is
    trust on first download: it trusts the build the buildbot served,
    and the hash holds it to exactly that build; a changed file is a
    new hash and is asked about again. Where EmuSen tested a build, the
    line says whether this is that exact build, by its SHA-256; that is
    information for your decision, never a condition of it.

UPDATING AND ROLLING BACK
    cores update installs the build the index now offers for each core
    whose index line changed since it was installed, or for those named
    (one already current says so). The new build is staged as cores get
    stages one; the installed build is moved to /Cores/libretro/
    .previous/ and the new one renamed into place, and if any rename
    fails the installed build is put back. The new build is approved
    again before it runs. One earlier build is kept: cores rollback puts
    it back, keeping the newer one, so a second cores rollback returns
    to it. Both keep their approvals. The build kept before is dropped
    at the next update, and its approval revoked, since it now matches
    no file.

REMOVING
    cores rm removes a core's folder and its kept build, each by a
    rename into staging first, so it vanishes whole. Your saves and the
    core's settings are kept in EmuSen's own folders and stay; a console
    whose default was the core falls back to its first engine. A folder
    you put in /Cores/libretro yourself, with no NOTICE, is yours, and
    cores rm never removes it. The approvals the core was given are
    revoked.

THE DEFAULT ENGINE
    cores default <console> prints the engine a console's games open on,
    and cores default <console> <engine> sets it; --game <path> does the
    same for one game, - clearing its own choice so that it follows its
    console. These are the runtime's Engine setting, the same value the
    Cores window's Make Default, a game's options and the metadata
    editor's Alternative emulator change; the runtime refuses an engine
    the console does not run, in its own words.

    cores default, cores approve and the revocations need the settings
    service, which a program with EmuSen's runtime provides, as
    Mistress's shell does; elsewhere the command says it is missing (see
    man modules).

VERIFYING
    cores verify prints one line per installed core, its result first:

        ok          the core is the file its NOTICE records, and the
                    libretro host and proxy copies are this EmuSen's
        refreshed   as ok, after the named copies were made again
                    from this EmuSen, as after EmuSen is updated
        changed     the core is not the file its NOTICE records: it
                    needs approval again, and verify changes nothing
        added       a folder of your own, not checked

    Refreshing never touches the core itself, so its approval stands.
    NOTICE is information only: the approval is the hash recorded when
    you approved the core, which is compared before it starts.

    Only one command that changes /Cores/libretro runs at a time,
    across every EmuSen program; another is refused until it ends.

CONSOLES
    A core is placed under EmuSen's consoles by its information's
    database names, or its systemid where those are blank, as the
    systems table's libretro names place it.

JOBS
    Each command is a job of the core tool. One the line's budget cuts
    short goes on in the background as cores<n>: cores wait follows it,
    ps lists it, kill stops it.

EXAMPLES
    cores fetch
    cores ls --available
    cores ls --updates | wc -l
    cores ls --installed | grep GPL
    cores info pcsx2
    cores get pcsx2
    cores update
    cores rollback pcsx2
    cores verify | grep -v '^ok'
    cores approve pcsx2
    cores default "PlayStation 2" Sapphire
    cores default --game /home/Games/ps2/jak.chd -
    cores rm pcsx2
    for c in $(cores ls --updates | awk '{print $3}'); do cores info $c; done

SOURCES
    libretro, "buildbot.libretro.com nightly linux x86_64",
    https://buildbot.libretro.com/nightly/linux/x86_64/latest/ and its
    .index-extended, read 2026-10-31: one line per core zip, its date,
    the zip's CRC-32 and its name.
    libretro, "libretro-core-info", 00_example_libretro.info,
    https://github.com/libretro/libretro-core-info, read 2026-10-31: the
    keys of a core information file.
    PKWARE, "APPNOTE.TXT - .ZIP File Format Specification": the central
    directory's fields, among them the external attributes that mark a
    link.

